\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Retaining one path twice gives correlated links}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Take a fine graph with one edge from #{s} to #{t} and a
coarse graph with two parallel edges between those same
vertices. Choose the single fine edge as both coarse paths.
The [coarse path map](fdip-00MO) is
##{\mathcal B:G\to G^2,\qquad \mathcal B(U)=(U,U).}
Its image reference is the pushforward of Haar onto the
diagonal. For a nontrivial finite group, choose a proper
nonempty subset #{A\subset G} and put #{p=\mu_G(A)}.
Then
##{\rho(A\times A)=p,\qquad
 (\mu_G\otimes\mu_G)(A\times A)=p^2,}
with #{0<p<1}. Thus the two references differ despite
endpoint covariance.}

\p{The same argument works on any nontrivial compact group
using a Borel set of Haar mass strictly between zero and one.
Such a set exists: two distinct points have disjoint
nonempty open neighborhoods, each of positive Haar measure
by \ref{fdip-007B}. For an integrable weight #{w} on #{G},
the [image-density formula](fdip-00MQ) gives
#{f(u,u)=w(u)} almost everywhere on this diagonal reference.
A density against independent link Haar is not supplied by
that formula.}
